# Implementation Workflow

Follow this workflow before implementing a non-trivial feature or change.

## 1. Understand the requirement

- Identify the exact feature or behavior being requested.
- Locate the relevant section of `.cursor/docs/overview.md`.
- Extract the applicable business rules, states, validations, constraints, and side effects.
- Do not infer missing product behavior when the documentation does not support it.

## 2. Determine the technical impact

Consult `.cursor/docs/software-architecture.md` and identify:

- affected backend areas;
- affected frontend areas;
- persistence requirements;
- API requirements;
- validation requirements;
- testing requirements.

Do not create architecture merely because a new entity or endpoint exists.

## 3. Plan before editing

For meaningful changes:

- identify the smallest set of files and layers involved;
- preserve existing architectural boundaries;
- reuse existing patterns when they are appropriate;
- avoid premature abstractions.

For changes involving multiple business rules, verify that all affected rules are represented in the implementation plan.

## 4. Implement

- Keep responsibilities separated according to the architecture document.
- Keep business/application logic out of HTTP controllers and UI components when it belongs in services or other appropriate layers.
- Do not modify unrelated code.
- Do not add features outside the documented scope.

## 5. Validate

After implementation:

- run the relevant type checks;
- run lint/format checks when applicable;
- run relevant unit/API tests;
- run E2E tests for important user flows when applicable;
- verify both the main path and important invalid/edge states.

## 6. Review

Before considering the task complete, verify:

- the implementation matches `overview.md`;
- the implementation follows `software-architecture.md`;
- no unnecessary abstraction was introduced;
- no undocumented behavior was invented;
- tests cover the relevant behavior;
- existing functionality was not unintentionally changed.
